      "text": "Let's solve the inequality $\\frac{x^2}{x+1} \\ge \\frac{2}{x-1} + \\frac{5}{4}$. We will first eliminate the fractions by finding a common denominator for the right-hand side. The common denominator can be $4(x-1)(x+1)$. Then we can rewrite the inequality as $\\frac{x^2(x-1)(x+1)}{4(x-1)(x+1)} \\ge \\frac{8(x-1)}{4(x-1)(x+1)} + \\frac{5(x+1)}{4(x-1)(x+1)}$.\n\nSimplifying, we get\n\n$$\\frac{x^2(x^2-1)}{4(x-1)(x+1)} \\ge \\frac{8x-8 + 5x + 5}{4(x-1)(x+1)}.$$\n\nSimplifying the fractions, we get\n\n$$\\frac{x^2(x+1)(x-1)}{4(x-1)(x+1)} \\ge \\frac{13x - 3}{4(x-1)(x+1)}.$$\n\n$$x^2(x-1)(x+1) \\ge 4(x-1)(x+1)(13x-3)$$\n\n$$x^2(x+1)(x-1) \\ge 4(13x^2 - 3x - 13x + 3)$$\n\n$$x^2(x^2 - 1) \\ge 4(13x^2 - 16x - 3)$$\n\n$$x^4 - x^2 \\ge 52x^2 - 64x - 12$$\n\n$$x^4 - 53x^2 + 64x + 12 \\ge 0.$$\n\nNow we have to find the roots of this equation, we can find the factors. The polynomial factors into \\[(x^2 - 64)(x^2 + 1) = 0.\\]\n\nSetting each factor to zero, we have two equations to solve:\n\n$x^2 - 64 = 0$ and $x^2 + 1 = 0$,\n\nSolving for $x$, we have $x = \\pm 8$ and $x = \\pm i$.\n\nTherefore, we have the real roots $x = -8, 8$ and imaginary roots $x = i$ and $x = -i$.\n\nNow that we have the roots, we can determine the intervals where the polynomial is positive. The roots $x = \\pm 8$ and $x = \\pm i$ are all real. By testing the intervals, we can see that $x^4 - 53x^2 + 64x + 12$ is positive at $x = -8$ and $x = 8$. The negative values of the polynomial don't matter as long as they don't intersect the roots.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{x \\in \\{-8, 8\\}}.$",
