      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x^2}{x+1} \\ge \\frac{2}{x-1} + \\frac{5}{4}\\), we first aim to find a common denominator to combine the terms on the right side. The common denominator is \\(4(x+1)(x-1)\\). Rewriting the inequality, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x+1} \\ge \\frac{8(x+1)}{4(x+1)(x-1)} + \\frac{5(x-1)}{4(x+1)(x-1)}.\n\\]\n\nCombining the terms on the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x+1} \\ge \\frac{8(x+1) + 5(x-1)}{4(x+1)(x-1)}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the numerator on the right side:\n\n\\[\nx^2 \\ge \\frac{8x + 8 + 5x - 5}{4(x+1)(x-1)} = \\frac{13x + 3}{4(x+1)(x-1)}.\n\\]\n\nSince the number line before the fraction has the form \\(3 = 0\\) and \\(-1 = 0\\), we can extend this to \\((x-1) \\neq 0,\\,(x+1) \\neq 0\\) and \\(1 \\neq 0\\). Therefore, this simplifies to \\(x \\neq -1, x \\neq 1, x \\neq 3\\).\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the inequality by \\(4(x+1)(x-1)\\):\n\n\\[\n4x(x-1) \\ge 13x + 3.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and rearranging terms gives:\n\n\\[\n4x^2 - 4x - 13x - 3 \\ge 0 \\implies 4x^2 - 17x - 3 \\ge 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve the equality \\(4x^2 - 17x - 3 = 0\\) to find the critical points. Using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{289 + 48}}{8} = \\frac{17 \\pm \\sqrt{337}}{8} = \\frac{17 \\pm 18.37}{8} = \\frac{25.37}{8}, \\frac{-1.37}{8}.\n\\]\n\nThese solutions are approximately \\(x \\approx 3.171\\) and \\(x \\approx -0.171\\). Therefore, the critical points are \\(x = \\frac{25 + \\sqrt{337}}{8},\\; x = \\frac{-17 + \\sqrt{337}}{8},\\; x = \\frac{-17 - \\sqrt{337}}{8},\\; x = \\frac{17 + \\sqrt{337}}{8},\\; x = \\frac{-17 - \\sqrt{337}}{8},\\; x = \\frac{17 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}\\).\n\nFinally, we test these values in the original inequality and find the solution sets. The solution set for \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\left(-\\infty, \\frac{17 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}\\right] \\cup \\left[\\frac{25 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}, \\infty\\right).\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(-\\infty, \\frac{25 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}\\right] \\cup \\left[\\frac{17 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}, \\frac{25 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}\\right] \\cup \\left(\\frac{25 + \\sqrt{337}}{8}, \\infty\\right)}.\n\\]",
